# Line endings are LF in the repository and LF in the working tree.
#
# Why this file exists: a PL/pgSQL function body is stored verbatim in
# pg_proc.prosrc. Without a rule here, core.autocrlf decides what a checkout
# looks like, so the .sql files are CRLF on a Windows tree and LF everywhere
# else - and the same migration then installs a textually different database
# depending on who ran it. Anything written across a line break, or any
# character class, means one thing on one checkout and another on the next, and
# no machine can see the difference because each one only ever builds one of the
# two. That is not hypothetical: it silently changed what
# rbac.validate_oauth_scopes returned, and both test suites stayed green.
#
# The loaders normalize to LF as they read (packages/cli/commands/migrate.ts,
# packages/cli/commands/extension.ts, scripts/bundle-sql.ts), which is what
# guarantees the installed database is identical either way and protects
# consumers who never check this repository out. This file is the other half:
# it keeps carriage returns out of the source in the first place, so nobody can
# type one into a string literal or a character class by accident.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shells are the exception: cmd.exe is not reliable with LF-only batch
# files, so these keep CRLF on checkout.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
